B2-SCGPT — owner: sg
=====================================================================
VERDICT: the bar is beaten. 0.8732 vs 0.8498, LOCTO, on real Kang IFN-beta.
         But read section 3 before calling it a scGPT win, because it isn't.
=====================================================================

1. WIRING (this is all vps needs)
---------------------------------
    from bricks.cell_scgpt import ScGPTCellModel
    model = ScGPTCellModel(bench)              # bench = data.kang.to_benchmark()

  constructor : ScGPTCellModel(benchmark, similarity="control",
                               checkpoint="blood", temperature=None, floor=True)
  class name  : ScGPTCellModel
  import path : bricks.cell_scgpt
  model.name  : "cell:control-sim-transfer(LOCTO,blood)"

  Implements BOTH interfaces, same as bricks/cell_transfer.py:
    - PerturbationModel : predict(control_mean, cell_type) -> perturbed_mean
    - Stage             : __call__(state) -> state, writes state["cell_delta"]
                          as {cell_type: delta_vector}

  It is a drop-in replacement for CellTransferModel — same constructor shape
  (takes the benchmark), same two interfaces, no new dependencies. Nothing to
  install: the DEFAULT path needs only numpy and the benchmark. Do not wire the
  scGPT path (see 3); it needs a 156 MB checkpoint and a second venv to score
  no better.

  Reproduce the scorecard:   python -m bricks.cell_scgpt
  Tests:                     python -m pytest tests/test_cell_scgpt.py -q   (8 pass)


2. WHAT THE MODEL IS
--------------------
  The mean-shift null averages every training cell type's delta with equal
  weight — it insists a megakaryocyte responds to IFN-beta like a monocyte.
  This model keeps the averaging and *weights* it by cell-state similarity:

      w_j  = softmax_j( sim(X, j) / T )     over training cell types j != X
      dhat = sum_j w_j * unit(delta_j)      unit-normalized so one high-amplitude
                                            cell type can't hijack the direction
      pred = max(dhat * scale, -control_X)  log1p data is >= 0, so a gene sitting
                                            at zero in X cannot go down

  `sim` comes from CONTROL data only. The floor is not a fudge — the null
  cheerfully predicts negative expression for genes a cell type doesn't express,
  and pays for it. Temperature T is picked per fold by an INNER leave-one-out
  over the 7 training cell types; it lands on 0.05 in every fold.

  Honesty properties, all of them checked and not merely asserted:
    - LOCTO is enforced and TESTED: tests/test_cell_scgpt.py corrupts a cell
      type's perturbed profile into noise and asserts its prediction is
      bit-identical. Same test for the selected temperature.
    - Nested selection: the reported number for a fold was not tuned on it.
    - Negative control: scramble which similarity goes with which training delta
      and the model collapses to 0.7828 — BELOW the plain leave-one-out mean
      shift (0.8232). The gain is the similarity, not a leak.


3. WHAT scGPT ACTUALLY CONTRIBUTED — the part that must not get rounded up
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  scGPT was the primary swing. It was run for real, and it works. It did not
  earn the headline.

    similarity source                aggregate delta_pearson
    global mean shift (the bar)                0.8498
    scGPT-blood cell embeddings                0.8696   beats the bar
    control-profile Pearson                    0.8732   beats the bar
    ensemble of the two                        0.8726
    noise ceiling (perfect model)              0.9075

  scGPT cell embeddings BEAT THE BAR. They do not beat plain Pearson
  correlation of the control mean profiles — a metric that costs nothing.
  Paired over the 8 folds: mean difference -0.0036, Wilcoxon p=0.55. There is
  no detectable difference between a 156 MB pretrained transformer and
  np.corrcoef here.

  scGPT's pretrained GENE embeddings contributed nothing at all. Two smoother
  formulations (rank-512 linear kernel, sparse k=16 kNN) both degraded the score
  monotonically, and the nested selection set their weight to zero in all 8
  folds. It blurs the interferon signature across genes scGPT thinks are
  related, which is exactly the specificity the bar is testing for.

  So: "a cell-state transfer model beats the null" is supported.
      "scGPT beats the null" is ALSO literally true (0.8696 > 0.8498) but
      misleading if said alone, because the free baseline does it better.
      "scGPT gave us the win" is FALSE. Do not put that in the deck.

  The scGPT path is kept and reproducible rather than deleted, so the negative
  result stays falsifiable.


4. scGPT ENGINEERING — what was built, and what did NOT validate
----------------------------------------------------------------
  The `scgpt` package pins flash-attn and an old torch and fights this
  CPU/aarch64 box, so scripts/scgpt_model.py re-implements the encoder in plain
  torch and loads `best_model.pt` by name. Architecture was read off scGPT's own
  source, not guessed (see the module docstring for the line-by-line mapping).
  Every encoder parameter in the checkpoint is matched; only the unused
  perturbation-flag and MVC heads are left over.

  VALIDATED:
    - gene embeddings involve no forward pass, so they are faithful by
      construction; biologically, the 20-gene ISG set is far more coherent than
      random gene sets (mean pairwise cosine +0.21 vs +0.02 +/- 0.01, z=+17.5).
    - the ENCODER: <cls> embeddings of Kang control cells give 94.3%
      leave-one-out 1-NN cell-type accuracy (chance 12.5%), cosine silhouette
      0.34, and recover immune lineage structure — myeloid block, T/NK block,
      megakaryocytes as the outlier.

  DID NOT VALIDATE — stated plainly because it bounds what we can claim:
    - the MLM expression DECODER head. Asked to reconstruct masked expression
      bins it regresses nearly to a constant (pearson r ~0.11 blood / 0.27
      human; prediction sd ~1 against a true bin sd of ~14). Cause not
      established: it may be a subtle mismatch in the re-implementation that we
      failed to find, or these re-hosted heads may not be the MLM head at
      inference scale. We therefore use ONLY the encoder and the gene embedding
      table, never `decoder`. Nothing in the scorecard depends on it.
      If anyone later wants scGPT's *generative* perturbation prediction
      (GEARS-style), this is the blocker to clear first.


5. WHAT THIS RESULT IS NOT
--------------------------
  BUILT != VALIDATED. This is one backtest, one dataset, one perturbation
  (IFN-beta on PBMCs), 8 cell types. It is NOT "we can predict MS drugs."

  Three specific limits worth carrying into any writeup:

  a) The bar is leaky in its own favour and we did not quietly fix it to look
     better. PerturbationBenchmark.global_mean_delta() averages the true delta
     over ALL cell types including the held-out one, so the null we beat has
     seen data our model never gets. The fair leave-one-out mean shift is
     0.8232. We beat the leaky version anyway; that is the harder comparison
     and it's the one reported.

  b) One of the 8 folds is unscoreable. Megakaryocytes have 63 control / 69
     stimulated cells and a split-half delta reliability of 0.058 — that fold is
     scoring measurement noise, for every model including the null, and it drags
     every aggregate down by roughly 0.05. Run scripts/noise_ceiling.py.
     On the 7 measurable cell types: bar 0.9032, model 0.9293, ceiling 0.9897 —
     the model closes ~30% of the gap between the null and what is achievable.

  c) With 8 folds the statistics are suggestive, not conclusive. The model wins
     7/8 cell types, but a paired Wilcoxon against the bar gives p=0.055 (and
     n=8 cannot produce better than p=0.008 even on a clean sweep). The
     aggregate margin is real; do not dress it as significance.

  It loses one cell type outright: Dendritic cells, 0.915 vs the bar's 0.932.


6. REPRODUCING THE scGPT PATH (optional, not needed to wire)
------------------------------------------------------------
    python3 -m venv .venv-scgpt && . .venv-scgpt/bin/activate
    pip install --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu torch
    pip install "numpy<2.1" scipy anndata scanpy
    # checkpoints (gitignored, ~156 MB / ~205 MB):
    #   https://huggingface.co/perturblab/scgpt-blood  -> data/cache/scgpt/blood/
    #   https://huggingface.co/perturblab/scgpt-human  -> data/cache/scgpt/human/
    python scripts/scgpt_embed.py --ckpt data/cache/scgpt/blood --n-per-type 128

  Writes data/cache/scgpt_embeddings_blood.npz (gitignored). ~25 min on 4 CPU
  cores. Then `similarity="scgpt"` in ScGPTCellModel picks it up. The heavy env
  never touches the pinned .venv — the brick reads the npz with plain numpy.

  Files owned by this lane (nothing else was modified except a one-line
  .gitignore entry for .venv-scgpt/):
    bricks/cell_scgpt.py        the brick
    scripts/scgpt_model.py      scGPT encoder re-implementation + validation notes
    scripts/scgpt_data.py       Kang -> scGPT tokenization (binning, <cls>, padding)
    scripts/scgpt_embed.py      embedding extraction CLI (.venv-scgpt only)
    scripts/noise_ceiling.py    split-half reliability / attainable ceiling
    tests/test_cell_scgpt.py    8 invariant tests incl. the leakage test
